sequenceDiagram
    participant FE as Frontend (Angular/RxJS)
    participant BE as Backend (Spring Boot)
    participant HMRC_Auth as HMRC Authorization Server
    participant Browser as User Browser

    activate FE
    FE->>BE: 1. Request Authorization URL (GET /api/hmrc/auth/start)
    deactivate FE

    activate BE
    BE->>BE: 1.1. Generate state parameter (CSRF protection)
    BE->>BE: 1.2. Construct HMRC Authorization URL\n(Includes client_id, redirect_uri, scope, state)
    BE->>FE: 2. Return Authorization URL: { "authUrl": "..." }
    deactivate BE

    activate FE
    FE->>Browser: 3. Receive URL & Trigger Browser Redirection
    deactivate FE

    Browser->>HMRC_Auth: 4. Browser Navigates to HMRC Auth URL

    activate HMRC_Auth
    HMRC_Auth->>Browser: 5. Prompt User for Login / Consent
    Browser->>HMRC_Auth: 6. User Logs In and Grants Authorization
    HMRC_Auth->>Browser: 7. Authorization Success, Redirect to BE Callback URL\n(URL includes code and state)
    deactivate HMRC_Auth

    Browser->>BE: 8. BE Callback Endpoint Hit (GET /api/hmrc/auth/callback?code=...&state=...)

    activate BE
    BE->>BE: 9. Validate received state parameter (Anti-CSRF/Replay)
    BE->>HMRC_Auth: 10. Exchange Code for Token (POST /token)\n(Includes code, client_id, **client_secret**)

    activate HMRC_Auth
    HMRC_Auth->>BE: 11. Return Access Token and Refresh Token
    deactivate HMRC_Auth

    BE->>BE: 12. **Securely Store Access/Refresh Token in DB** (Link to User)
    BE->>Browser: 13. Redirect to FE Success Page (e.g., /auth/success)
    deactivate BE

    activate FE
    Browser->>FE: 14. Browser hits FE Success Page
    FE->>FE: 15. Route Handles Success, User is Authorized
    FE->>BE: 16. Subsequent Business Request (BE uses Stored Token to call HMRC API)
    deactivate FE